        "coleoidea": "Coleoidea",
        "coleoidea-περιγραφή-1": "The Coleoidea differ from the nautilus and other types of cephalopods. They possess an internal shell or no shell at all, allowing for a flexible and agile body. They have eight or ten arms, most of which are freed from a benthic lifestyle. They do not move with feet, but use jet propulsion, rapidly changing color and shape. They manipulate light and shadow with exceptional skill, sometimes generating internal light. They exhibit remarkable intelligence and memory, surpassing all other invertebrates. They are skilled hunters, masters of camouflage and adaptation.",
        
        "coleoidea-ετυμολογία-1": "“Coleoidea” from Greek κολεός (koleós, “sheath, scabbard”) + suffix -οειδής, i.e. “sheathed” — referring to the internalized, mantle-enclosed shell (gladius, phragmocone, or rostrum), in contrast to the external shell of nautiloids. The subclass was established by Bather in 1888.",
        

